00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.120
in the description below. But anyway, getting back&nbsp;
to the video, hearing all of this, some of you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.040
probably wondering: what exactly even is a bond?&nbsp;
Well, bonds, just like stocks, are a way to invest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.920
in a company’s future. But instead of buying a&nbsp;
piece of the company, bonds are a way to help fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.880
the company. When you buy a bond, the money you&nbsp;
invest will be given to the company, government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.960
university, or foundation of your choice. These&nbsp;
institutions will then turn around and use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.160
capital to fund expansion efforts, research and&nbsp;
development, public projects, new infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.320
and whatever else they think would grow profits&nbsp;
and revenue or serve the community depending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.520
on whether they’re for profit or not. And in&nbsp;
return for helping them accomplish their goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.720
these institutions will pay you back with&nbsp;
interest over a set period of time. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.800
you get an interest payment every quarter or every&nbsp;
6 months until maturity at which point you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:01.960
your initial investment back which is generally&nbsp;
$1000. To be honest, it’s a pretty simple system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:08.120
and that’s why it’s been around for millennia. In&nbsp;
fact, bonds are arguably the oldest investment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:14.440
all time dating all the way back to 2400 BC&nbsp;
to the Mesopotamians. Instead of cash though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:19.040
bond investors back in the day were paid&nbsp;
back with corn. If you’re a Shakespeare fan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.880
you’ve probably also heard of the famous saying&nbsp;
“a pound of flesh”. In Shakespeare's “Merchant of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.040
Venice”, the main character would essentially&nbsp;
promise that if he doesn’t pay back his bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:32.880
the villain can take a pound of his flesh.&nbsp;
The bond market has come a long way since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.440
those medieval days though. In fact, today, the&nbsp;
bond market is the world’s largest securities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:44.680
market. The US bond market is worth $51.3&nbsp;
trillion and the global bond market is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:52.280
$133 trillion dollars. For perspective, the global&nbsp;
stock market is “only” worth $109 trillion. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:59.560
if the bond market is so large and so established,&nbsp;
why is everyone suddenly piling in right now?

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:08.760
HYPE EXPLAINED:
Well, the most obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:14.080
factor is none other than the high interest rate&nbsp;
environment. After 15 years of keeping interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:20.720
rates at near zero levels, the Federal Reserve&nbsp;
has jacked up rates all the way to 5.33%. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:26.200
has led to bond interest rates skyrocketing&nbsp;
across the board. In fact, almost everyday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:31.640
we see headlines about how bond yields just&nbsp;
reached 16 years highs. In general, the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:38.800
government is paying 4.5 to 5.5% on treasury&nbsp;
bills, notes, and bonds. Top companies like Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:45.040
Google, Microsoft, and Facebook are paying 5-6%.&nbsp;
And if you look close enough, you can even find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.560
investment grade bonds from companies like&nbsp;
Kelloggs, Discover, Paramount, and Time Warner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:57.280
Cable that are paying 7-8%, and this right here is&nbsp;
what’s enticing a lot of investors. If you didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:03.440
notice, that’s nearly the same return as the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500 over the past 50 years which is generally the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.960
benchmark for gauging investment returns. And now,&nbsp;
investors are able to get nearly the same yields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:15.120
with bonds which are in general far less volatile&nbsp;
and more reliable than stocks. Now, let me preface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.800
by saying that there is no such thing as a risk&nbsp;
free investment. All investments carry risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:27.200
that’s kind of why they generate a return in&nbsp;
the first place. But, in the spectrum of risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:33.160
US government bonds are as close as you’re gonna&nbsp;
get when it comes to minimizing risk. In fact, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:38.320
only time that a financial institution is allowed&nbsp;
to describe an investment as risk free is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.400
they’re referring to treasury yields. Treasury&nbsp;
yields are considered “risk free” because they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:49.480
backed by the US federal government who in theory&nbsp;
can just print money to pay back bond holders. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:54.160
when investors are able to lock in 5% yields not&nbsp;
just for as long as their savings account pays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:00.840
high interest but for the next 5, 10, 20, 30 years&nbsp;
with the federal government, it’s no wonder why so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:06.360
many investors like Warren Buffett are taking the&nbsp;
plunge. But, it’s not just about locking in high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.840
rates. For many investors, buying bonds has to&nbsp;
do with protecting against inflation. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.840
you’re gonna fare a lot better against inflation&nbsp;
if your cash is growing at 5% but there’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:22.640
an even more interesting way to protect against&nbsp;
inflation from the federal government themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.760
and it’s called TIPS or Treasury Inflation&nbsp;
Protected Securities. Unlike traditional bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.760
where you lock in a specific yield when you buy,&nbsp;
TIPS have a variable return that’s directly linked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:38.200
to inflation. The value of the bond essentially&nbsp;
rises with the current inflation rate. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:42.360
if we were to have a year where there was&nbsp;
9% inflation, the value of the bond would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:49.040
also rise by 9% that year. In the off chance that&nbsp;
there’s somehow deflation, your bond value stays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:53.720
the same. Obviously, this doesn’t provide&nbsp;
any real return, but it ensures that your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:58.800
cash isn’t being eaten up by inflation either.&nbsp;
Hearing all these factors alone, I think you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.840
piece together why all of these big investors&nbsp;
and companies are buying bonds. But, when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:10.200
combine this with a stock market slump, well&nbsp;
all of this just goes into overdrive. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:16.520
2 year treasury yields are almost equal to the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500’s trailing 12 month’s earning yield. So, a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:23.200
of investors are simply migrating from stocks to&nbsp;
bonds. But, if the bond market is so large and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.240
established, and now also really appealing, why&nbsp;
do we as retail investors never hear about bonds?

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:39.080
THE HIDDEN MARKET:
Well, the mainstream answer is that interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:46.120
rates have simply been low over the past 20 years&nbsp;
rendering bonds not that attractive but honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:52.720
this is a BS answer because companies and&nbsp;
institutions never stopped investing in bonds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:58.360
Sure, they hold more bonds now than ever but they&nbsp;
were already holding a tens of billions if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:03.360
hundreds of billions of dollars worth of bonds&nbsp;
even during low interest rate periods. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:09.440
here’s a graph of how the US Corporate bond market&nbsp;
has grown over the past 40 years. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:14.520
it’s just been straight up regardless of interest&nbsp;
rates and this makes perfect sense if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.560
subscribe to the efficient market hypothesis.&nbsp;
The efficient market hypothesis states that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:26.040
security prices reflect all known information&nbsp;
at any given time. This means that if bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:31.640
truly were unappealing at some point in time, the&nbsp;
market would simply sell off until bonds reached a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:37.440
normal level of desirability. Similarly, if bonds&nbsp;
truly are underappreciated right now, the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:43.120
would simply buy up bonds until a normal level&nbsp;
of desirability is achieved. And this doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:48.680
just apply to bonds or even just investments in&nbsp;
general. It’s the same thing with grocery prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:53.280
and restaurant prices and tuition prices and&nbsp;
basically anything else that’s governed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.960
forces of supply and demand. While something may&nbsp;
be overvalued or undervalued at a given point in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:05.760
time, over the long term, all investments maintain&nbsp;
an equilibrium between themselves. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:12.120
if you’re choosing between bonds and dogecoin,&nbsp;
there is no objective winner. Each investment is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.000
equally desirable and their respective returns&nbsp;
are simply in line with their respective risk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:23.120
This is why companies never stopped investing in&nbsp;
bonds. Bonds simply always had a place in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:30.160
portfolio just like high risk acquisitions and the&nbsp;
Metaverse. But, if that’s the case, why are bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.800
so unpopular with retail investors? Well, I think&nbsp;
this has more to do with optics and the goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:42.320
of brokerages as opposed to the desirability or&nbsp;
undesirability of bonds. Here’s the thing, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:48.560
were a brokerage, what’s easier to pitch? Invest&nbsp;
$100 into this hot new NFT and have a chance at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:55.720
making $10,000 in 2 months or invest $10,000&nbsp;
into this treasury bill and reliably earn $500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:02.360
a year. It’s obviously the former. Sure, it might&nbsp;
sound a bit sketchy, but at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:09.280
it’s only a $100, so many people just treat it as&nbsp;
a sort of lottery ticket as opposed to methodical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:14.480
investing. If you've ever watched Wolf of Wall&nbsp;
Street, you might remember the scene where Jordan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:19.400
Belfort tried to sell a penny stock "Aerotyne&nbsp;
International" by describing it as a "cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:24.080
edge high tech firm" that was "awaiting patent&nbsp;
approval" and that a "6,000 investment would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:34.560
turn 60,000". While brokerages aren't nearly&nbsp;
as predatory, this shows you a bit more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.880
where their interests lie when they’re pushing&nbsp;
stock trading, options trading, crypto trading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:45.240
forex trading, futures trading, NFTs, web3,&nbsp;
you name it. For modern brokerages, it’s simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:50.880
been a lot easier to entice people with high&nbsp;
returns than solid investing principles. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:56.120
not only has it been easier to entice people with&nbsp;
high returns, it’s also been more profitable. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:00.760
obvious reasons, people constantly trading&nbsp;
a bunch of stocks, options and crypto is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:06.360
lot more profitable for brokerages than people&nbsp;
buying a bond and holding it for 2 years. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:11.920
in the name of maximizing users and revenue,&nbsp;
brokerages have largely sidelined bonds over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:17.560
the past few decades. But, don’t be fooled, the&nbsp;
biggest companies in the world aren’t throwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:23.880
trillions of dollars at doge coin, they’re&nbsp;
throwing it at bonds. So, whether it’s on Silo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:29.200
or some other platform, I would encourage you to&nbsp;
research bonds because it’s more than likely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:34.360
these companies know what they’re doing. Speaking&nbsp;
of companies knowing what they’re doing, Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:39.360
has been secretly taking control of the entire&nbsp;
world’s code. Check out this video to learn more.

